% ============================================================================
% long-bone-parts — the defining descriptor the source gives each named region
% of a long bone (adj-facts-stdlib, ANATOMY).
% ============================================================================
% A skeletal-microanatomy fact on the way to medicine: a long bone (femur,
% humerus, phalanx) is not a uniform rod — it is built from NAMED regions, and
% the source hands each one a single defining descriptor. From the middle
% outward and end-ward:
%
%     diaphysis        — the SHAFT (the long tubular middle)
%     epiphysis        — the TIP of the bone (each rounded articulating end)
%     metaphysis       — the region BETWEEN diaphysis and epiphysis
%     periosteum       — the membrane that SURROUNDS the bone surface
%     epiphyseal_plate — the cartilage responsible for LINEAR (length) GROWTH
%
% This complements — and does NOT overlap — the sibling anatomy tables. Those
% map bones to which body region they belong to (foot/hand groupings) or list
% organs; this table is the INTERNAL microanatomy of a single long bone, a
% different axis of fact entirely.
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? long_bone_part(diaphysis, $D)     % shaft
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `long_bone_part(part, description)` carrying its own row's citation,
% so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% descriptor WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on
% anything that is not one of these named regions (it never invents one).
%
% The five regions, as a little truth table:
%
%     part               description                      what the source says
%     ----------------   ------------------------------   --------------------------
%     diaphysis          shaft                            "Also known as the shaft"
%     epiphysis          tip_of_bone                      "at the tip of the long bone"
%     metaphysis         between_diaphysis_and_epiphysis  "between the diaphysis and epiphysis"
%     periosteum         surrounds_bone_surface           "surrounds the bone surface"
%     epiphyseal_plate   linear_bone_growth               "responsible for linear bone growth"
%
% Why these tokens: each `description` atom is built from the ONE phrase the
% source states verbatim about that region — the source literally calls the
% diaphysis "the shaft", places the epiphysis "at the tip of the long bone",
% names the metaphysis "the region between the diaphysis and epiphysis", says
% "the periosteum surrounds the bone surface", and states epiphyseal plates are
% "responsible for linear bone growth". No token is a paraphrase the source
% does not use, so every value is re-checkable against the spans below. (One
% quote in this comment is not byte-exact and cannot be: wherever it reads
% "surrounds the bone surface", the page has a NON-BREAKING SPACE, U+00A0,
% between "surrounds" and "the". The shipped `source` carries it.)
%
% Why the MEDULLARY CAVITY (the marrow-filled hollow center) is NOT a row: the
% chosen primary source lists "medullary cavity" only as a label in an
% illustration caption — it never states a defining sentence for it — so
% grounding a medullary_cavity → contains_marrow row from THIS source would be
% inventing wording the source does not give. Per byte-provenance it is DROPPED
% rather than fabricated; a future revision citing a source that defines it
% cleanly can add it back.
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a descriptor and recall the region:
%
%     ? long_bone_part($P, shaft)              % diaphysis — reverse recall
%
% Something that is not a named region of a long bone — a tendon, a ligament, a
% muscle, the whole femur — has no row, so a recall on it ABSTAINS rather than
% inventing a descriptor. That honest-abstention property is what makes the
% table safe to reason from before an orthopedics or fracture-classification
% agent reasons up to fracture LOCATION (which is named precisely by WHICH of
% these regions is broken — a diaphyseal vs. metaphyseal vs. epiphyseal
% fracture).
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every part→description pair
% was read out of a fetched NIH / NCBI Bookshelf page this session, and any pair
% that could not be grounded there was dropped, e.g. medullary_cavity above).
% All five pairs come from the SAME primary reference, the NIH National Library
% of Medicine's NCBI Bookshelf StatPearls chapter "Anatomy, Bones" (NBK537199),
% each quoted here so any reader can re-check it -- character-for-character
% except one: the page writes a NON-BREAKING SPACE (U+00A0) between
% "surrounds" and "the" in the periosteum sentence, which a comment cannot
% show; the shipped `source` carries it. The
% page introduces the first three with: "Long bones have 3 distinct anatomical
% zones:".
%
%   diaphysis → shaft
%     "Diaphysis: Also known as the shaft."
%
%   epiphysis → tip_of_bone
%     "Epiphysis: Located at the tip of the long bone, typically responsible for
%      articulation."
%
%   metaphysis → between_diaphysis_and_epiphysis
%     "Metaphysis: The region between the diaphysis and epiphysis that contains
%      the epiphyseal plate in children."
%
%   periosteum → surrounds_bone_surface
%     "The periosteum surrounds the bone surface."
%
%   epiphyseal_plate → linear_bone_growth
%     "Epiphyseal plates are responsible for linear bone growth and remain
%      cartilaginous until after puberty."
%
% EACH ROW CARRIES ITS OWN SENTENCE AS ITS `source` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e, #14986).
% This paragraph used to say that an ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance
% envelope, and so held the diaphysis line as the envelope -- which made it the
% primary source of the epiphysis, metaphysis, periosteum and epiphyseal-plate
% answers, none of which it describes. The language has per-row blocks.
%
% `source`, not `cites`: every sentence names its own part. The Diaphysis,
% Epiphysis and Metaphysis lines are each ONE list item whose "Term:" head is
% inline in the same element (not a separate heading), and the other two name
% their subject in running prose.
%
% ONE SPAN WAS NEVER ON THE PAGE. The periosteum sentence was shipped with an
% ordinary space between "surrounds" and "the"; the page writes
% `&#x000a0;`, a NON-BREAKING SPACE. That string occurs zero times in a
% rendering that keeps U+00A0; a renderer that collapses all whitespace hides
% the difference. The shipped `source` now carries the page's character.
%
% The envelope is now "Long bones evolve via endochondral ossification.", from
% the same section, which names no part. Every row overrides it, so it frames
% the table and warrants no answer. Measured 2026-09-15: each row sentence and
% the envelope occur exactly once. All five rows share the ONE source — NCBI
% Bookshelf StatPearls, a peer-reviewed reference hosted and distributed by the
% NIH National Library of Medicine (ncbi.nlm.nih.gov, a primary U.S. government
% host) — so `trust authoritative`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts, feedback_no_byte_arithmetic_for_llm.)
% ============================================================================

table long_bone_part {
    columns part, description

    row (diaphysis, shaft) {
        source "Diaphysis: Also known as the shaft."
    }
    row (epiphysis, tip_of_bone) {
        source "Epiphysis: Located at the tip of the long bone, typically responsible for articulation."
    }
    row (metaphysis, between_diaphysis_and_epiphysis) {
        source "Metaphysis: The region between the diaphysis and epiphysis that contains the epiphyseal plate in children."
    }
    row (periosteum, surrounds_bone_surface) {
        % U+00A0 between "surrounds" and "the", as on the page.
        source "The periosteum surrounds the bone surface."
    }
    row (epiphyseal_plate, linear_bone_growth) {
        source "Epiphyseal plates are responsible for linear bone growth and remain cartilaginous until after puberty."
    }

    % THE ENVELOPE WAS THE DIAPHYSIS LINE, and so the primary source of
    % every other answer. Each row now carries its own sentence; this
    % framing sentence names no part.
    source "Long bones evolve via endochondral ossification."
    locator "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537199/"
    trust authoritative
}
